# [[ .Name ]]

A [tug](https://github.com/cuonggt/tug) app: Go handlers render [[ .Framework ]] pages
through Inertia, with no API in between
[[- if .Database ]], and what it keeps is in
[[ .DatabaseName ]]
[[- end ]]. `README.md` says what each file
is. This says how to change the app as tug has it done, what not to do,
and how to check a change. tug's guide, at the version `go.mod` requires,
is at [[ .Guide ]].

## Check a change

Before saying a change is done:

```sh
[[ if or .SQLite (not .Database) -]]
tug gen            # the TypeScript of the Go's pages and routes, which tug dev writes too
npm run typecheck  # the pages against the Go's props, routes and forms
go fmt ./...       # the Go as gofmt writes it
go vet ./...
go test ./...
[[- else -]]
docker compose up -d   # the database, which tug dev and the tests use
tug gen                # the TypeScript of the Go's pages and routes, which tug dev writes too
npm run typecheck      # the pages against the Go's props, routes and forms
go fmt ./...           # the Go as gofmt writes it
go vet ./...
go test ./...
[[- end ]]
```

`tug routes` lists the routes, each with the line that added it, and
`tug build` builds the frontend into one binary, as a deploy does. The
tests visit the pages as Inertia's client does, with `tugtest`, with no
browser or build
[[- if .Database ]], each on a database of its own
[[- end ]]: they're how a page is checked. A binary run by hand
takes `ADDR=127.0.0.1:8080`, as the default listens on every interface.

## See it running

To see the app as it runs, as how a page looks, run `tug dev` in the
background: it serves until it's stopped, at the address it prints,
`http://localhost:8080`, builds the app again as a file changes, and its
output is the app's log. Each request the app answers under it is kept
for Inertia's DevTools, as a file of JSON in `.tug/devtools`, named by
when it came, so the newest sorts last: its `__meta` has the request's
`url`, its `status` and the page's `component`, `propValues` has the
props the page was sent, and `route` the function that answered. A
browser of the agent's own sees the pages, and `curl` gets one, whose
entry is the newest then:

```sh
curl http://localhost:8080/
ls .tug/devtools | tail -1   # its entry
```
[[- if .Database ]]

`tug run` runs the app's commands with `.env` too, as
`tug run migrate status`, and `tug run help` lists them.
[[- end ]]

## Rules

- `resources/js/tug` is tug gen's, from the Go: never edit it. A type
  there that's wrong is changed in the Go, then written again by tug gen.
- The pages run under a Content-Security-Policy, which runs a script or a
  `<style>` only with the page's nonce, as `app.html`'s carry
  `{{ .Nonce }}`. Don't write an inline script, an `on...` attribute, or a
  `<style>` without it, or add `'unsafe-inline'` to the policy in
  `main.go`. A library that injects a style is handed the nonce, from the
  page's `<meta property="csp-nonce">`. What's blocked, the browser's
  console says, and the app's log.
- A route whose handler binds input says which, with `Takes`, and its
  form's action is `form()`'s, typed by that input.
- A flash key is declared in `flash.go`, with `tug.Flash`, and set through
  it.
[[- if .Database ]]
- A handler's writes are in one transaction, `data.inTx`'s, and so are the
  jobs it pushes about them, so they're kept together, or not at all. No
  slow work in it, which holds up the other writes.
- A migration that has run is never changed: the app stops as it starts
  at one whose SQL has. A change is a new one.
[[- end ]]
- An error says what's wrong to the person who gets it, as "title is
  required": no Go types, and no raw errors. What the app says to a person
  goes through `c.T`, for `tug lang` to find, to translate.
[[- if .SSR ]]
- A page renders on the server too, in Node: no `window` or `document` at
  a module's top, or before it's mounted.
[[- end ]]

## Add a page

1. In Go, its props, each named by its json tag, its declaration, and its
   handler, which gives it its title, as `home` does:

   ```go
   type PostsIndexProps struct {
   	Posts []Post `json:"posts"`
   }

   var PostsIndex = tug.Page[PostsIndexProps]("Posts/Index")

   func postsIndex(c *tug.Ctx) error {
   	c.Head(inertia.Title("Posts"))
   	return PostsIndex.Render(c, PostsIndexProps{Posts: posts})
   }
   ```

2. Its route, in `newApp`, named, as the frontend links to it by its name:

   ```go
   app.Get("/posts", postsIndex).Name("posts.index")
   ```

3. Its component, `resources/js/pages/<Name>.[[ .Component ]]`, here
   `Posts/Index`, in `Layout`, as `resources/js/pages/Home.[[ .Component ]]` is.
[[- if .Vue ]]
   Its props are `defineProps<Pages['Posts/Index'] & SharedProps>()`,
   from `resources/js/tug/pages.ts`, as Vue can't work out
   `PageProps<'Posts/Index'>`.
[[- else ]]
   Its props are `PageProps<'Posts/Index'>`, from
   `resources/js/tug/pages.ts`.
[[- end ]]
[[- if .Svelte ]]
   Its title is said again with `<Head title="Posts" />`, from
   `resources/js/Head.svelte`, as Svelte keeps the document's title by
   it.
[[- end ]]
4. A link to it is `route('posts.index')`, from
   `resources/js/tug/routes.ts`, which knows each named route, and the
   values of its wildcards, by name.

5. Its own words go through `useLang`, from `resources/js/lib/lang.ts`, as
   the home page's do: `t('Save')` says a word in the request's language,
   and `choice(':count post|:count posts', n)` says one for a count.
   `tug lang vi` writes them into `lang/<language>.json`, beside what the
   Go says, to translate, and `tug lang -check` lists what's left to
   translate in each language; a change is done before its translation is.
   `n(1234)` and `d(post.createdAt, { dateStyle: 'long' })` say a number
   and a time the way the language writes them: a time in the app's zone,
   `APP_TIMEZONE`'s, and in the reader's own once the page is open in
   their browser.

6. Its style, in `resources/css/app.css`, names a start and an end rather
   than a left and a right: `margin-inline-start`, `padding-inline-end`,
   `border-inline-start`, `text-align: start`. A page laid out that way
   mirrors itself in a language written right to left, which the document
   says with `dir`.

## Add a form

1. Its input, with validate's tags, and a handler that binds it:

   ```go
   type PostInput struct {
   	Title string `json:"title" validate:"required,max=100"`
   }

   func storePost(c *tug.Ctx) error {
   	var in PostInput
   	if err := c.BindValid(&in); err != nil {
   		return err // back to the form, with what to fix
   	}
   	// ...
   	Success.Set(c, c.T("Saved."))
   	return c.RedirectRoute("posts.index")
   }
   ```

2. Its route, which takes it:

   ```go
   app.Post("/posts", storePost).Name("posts.store").Takes(PostInput{})
   ```

[[ if .React -]]
3. The form is `<Form<Inputs['posts.store']> action={form('posts.store')}>`,
   whose errors are its input's fields, as
   `resources/js/pages/Home.tsx`'s is.
[[- else -]]
3. The form is a `<Form>` of `createForm<Inputs['posts.store']>()`, with
   `form('posts.store')` as its action, whose errors are its input's
   fields, as `resources/js/pages/Home.[[ .Component ]]`'s is.
[[- end ]]
   Its `validate('title')`, as a field is left, has the server check it,
   by the same `BindValid`.
[[- if .Database ]]

## Add a table

1. `tug migrate new create_posts` writes
   `migrations/<when>_create_posts.sql`: the table in [[ .DatabaseName ]]'s SQL,
   and after its `-- down` line, what undoes it. The app runs it as it
   starts, and under tug dev as it's written.
2. Its Go: the row's type, and the table's, with its queries in
   `<table>_db.go`, as `jobs.go` and `jobs_db.go` are, in [[ .DatabaseName ]]'s
   SQL, with [[ if .Postgres ]]`$1`[[ else ]]`?`[[ end ]] for each value, through its `db`, a `dbtx`, so
   they run in a transaction too:

   ```go
   type posts struct {
   	db dbtx
   }

   func (ps *posts) in(tx *sql.Tx) *posts {
   	return &posts{db: tx}
   }
   ```

3. Made in `newApp`, on `data.db`, the database, and handed to the
   handlers that use it, as methods of a type that holds it, or as their
   closures:

   ```go
   ps := &posts{db: data.db}
   app.Get("/posts", func(c *tug.Ctx) error { return postsIndex(c, ps) }).Name("posts.index")
   ```

4. Writes that go together, in `data.inTx`, through `ps.in(tx)`, with the
   jobs about them.

## Add a job

A job's kind is made in `newApp` with `queue.Handle`, on `data.queue`, as
`use` in `database.go` makes the jobs that prune the tables, and pushed
with its `Push`, or in a handler's transaction through
`Kind.In(data.jobs.in(tx))`. It carries what it needs to find its work, as
IDs, and does it as it runs. A job on a schedule is `Schedule`'s, as
`prune-jobs`. `./[[ .Name ]] jobs`, or `tug run jobs` in development, lists the
jobs that failed for good, and runs them again.
[[- end ]]

## Test it

[[ if .Database -]]
The tests are Go's, beside the files they test, as `main_test.go`, through
`newClient`, each on a database of its own, which `newTestApp` makes:
[[- else -]]
The tests are Go's, in `main_test.go`, through `newClient`:
[[- end ]]

```go
func TestThePostsPageListsThePosts(t *testing.T) {
	c := newClient(t)
	posts := tugtest.Props(c.Get("/posts"), PostsIndex)
	// ...
	r := c.Post("/posts", map[string]any{"title": ""})
	if errs := r.Follow().Errors(); errs["title"] != "title is required" {
		t.Fatalf("errors %v", errs)
	}
}
```

`tugtest.Props` fails the test for another page. A redirect's next page
is `r.Follow()`, its flash `r.Page.Flash`.

## Upgrade tug

`tug upgrade`, with nothing uncommitted, brings the app what the starter
has gained since the tug that made it, which `tug.json` names: it merges
the starter's changes into the app's own, and marks where both changed
the same lines, between `<<<<<<<` and `>>>>>>>`.
[[- if .Database ]] A migration the starter
changed is listed with its diff rather than changed, as one that has run
is never changed: make the same change in a new one, with `tug migrate
new`, in [[ .DatabaseName ]]'s SQL.
[[- end ]] Put right what it marks,
keeping what the app's change was for, then check the change as above.
